The standard electrode potential of aluminum is -1.662V, and the electrochemical equivalent is 0.3356g/(A-h). The density of aluminum is only 30.13% of that of copper, therefore, replacing copper with aluminum can significantly reduce the mass of the workpiece, thus saving energy and reducing greenhouse gas emissions.
“Aluminum instead of copper” does not mean using aluminum instead of copper in all fields and sectors, but mainly means using aluminum instead of copper to produce conductive and thermally conductive (heat dissipation) parts. Aluminum is about 70% lighter than copper, which reduces the mass of the workpiece and thus makes it easier to install.
It must be admitted that aluminum is inferior to copper in some respects. The electrical conductivity of aluminum is lower than that of copper.
