In those days, the horse was far from being a domesticated animal, and it was always seen as an imposing beast loaded with enormous physical qualities. This opposition to being retained by the orders of the human being, generated a predilection on the part of the man towards this animal, of which he envied its strong indomitable and wild spirit.
In addition, that rebellious spirit, of lock to be dominated has caused that for many other cultures, among them the oldest populations of North America, have in this animal the clearest example of freedom. A person with a horse tattooed on his skin is seen as free, unreachable, etc.
Contrary to everything that has been detailed in the previous section, with the passage of time the horse has become an obedient, trained animal and one of the best supports of man. Being so the most representative symbol of fidelity and loyalty.
Unicorn tattoos mean, fundamentally, magic, spirituality, fantasy, etc. It could be said that they are the faithful reflection of the achievement of the most utopian ideals that can overwhelm human reason and thought.
In fact, for these people the horse was synonymous with courage, imposing on the adversary, conquest, victory ... In short, the representation of this animal was translated as the maxim the most prestigious triumph, as a clear expression of success in its highest degree.
Horses have not always enjoyed the sympathy of man, who on occasion has dismissed them as something negative. If we move to Hindu society, the horse is a harbinger of damaging climate revolutions.
In their beliefs and religion, their god Buddha disappeared from the world, nothing more and nothing less, than on the back of a great white horse. And it is that Buddha for them is the most representative example and figure of the law and postulates to follow, which has derived that normally tattoos of that same white horse are made accompanied by what they call the 'Book of the law'.
Among all the signs of the Chinese zodiac, we can find horses. The tattoos of these animals in Chinese culture are associated with passion, emotional stability, admiration for the other person and, ultimately, love.
In many other cultures, man has always used the horse as a means of transport with which to bring his knowledge to other places, among many other things. This is the reason why the horse manifests itself as a figure or emblem of transmission of knowledge.
