To a covered two-wheeled cart of some more or less resistant fabric and a simple material it is called a Carromato.
It's a very large horse carriage, similar to the saloon but richly and laboriously adorned. He carried four flashlights, one in each corner and all sides closed with glass.
Today it is used by royal families for great celebrations.
Possibly one of the most used terms, along with carriage, to talk about horse carriages and even to define what they are, it is: carriage.
In the upper part they had a railing to house the luggage of the travelers. Behind the davit, and on the roof of the carriage and in front of the aforementioned railing, was the coupe, a transverse seat, open at the front and with space for three people.
Quite large carriage arranged on four wheels and inside which there are seats for six or eight people. The cover of a very resistant fabric was supported by wooden rings and reeds that emerged from the sides.
The box is attached to the front set with gooseneck fittings and there also has a mobile seat that rests on the fender, place for the driver. In the part of the pestante it can take a seat for the footman.
En Roman Empire times, the chariot was a chariot drawn by four horses in a line, hence its name. It was a means of transportation used by Roman generals when they entered the cities in triumph.
The variant with two horses instead of four, called biga, it was already used in the times of the Greeks and Egyptians.
Inside the horse carriages, the landau it is one of the most comfortable. Considered a luxury, it is a covered carriage whose box goes on four wheels.
The sulky or sulki is a small carriage used in rural areas from many corners of the world, to carry one or two passengers usually. It is of morphology and design simple and light.
