4 In short, how do horses mate?
The most suitable season of the year for the birth of young horses is spring, as it happens in many other animal species, especially in all those that belong to the group of mammals. The reason or the cause is that at this time the food is usually much more abundant and the weather conditions are not usually extreme but rather mild, with daylight hours playing a fundamental role.
A little trick, which other breeders of other domestic animals also use, using ornithologists as an example, is to provide artificial light in the barn or breeding place. This technique causes the biological clock of our horses to start and thus we can advance the reproductive period.
There are pregnancies in mares in which the foal has developed correctly and is born at the end of the first ten months, requiring a series of special care. On the contrary, there are situations in which this process has lasted until the year, usually when the offspring is a male.
It is important to say that the development of the young occurs inside the uterus in the last three months of pregnancy. Therefore, in the first six months of gestation, the mares can be covered without any problem, but from this moment on it can become a serious danger that can even cause the death of the young foal.
A tip for all those breeders whose animals are not in the open air, but remain housed in stables and small places for most of the day, is the exercise. Taking short walks, and even light jogs, to our pregnant mares is a very positive practice.
If we want our foal to grow strong and healthy, it is necessary to have a series of basic notions about their behavior, feeding habits, etc.
These long legs also prohibit it from reaching with its snout the grass located on the ground, which implies that, in a natural environment, the foals only feed on the milk produced by their mother. A milk rich in proteins that accelerate and improve the growth of the young.
Once the foal has finished its lactation, it is advisable to provide high quality feed to make up for any deficiencies in its development. It is advisable to give him some type of supplements, especially of a vitamin nature (vitamins A, D and E in particular).
If not, fatal mishaps can ensue. Despite being young, young horses are very active animals, so if we notice that our foal spends much of his time sleeping, it may be because he is weak or ill.
But the process is much more complex since in artisanal reproduction, not in insemination there are stages that horses and mares perform and that are necessary to achieve mating, since previously there must be a courtship moment, where the mare plays while the male dominates until at one point the mare fully shows her delivery and it is at that moment that mating occurs.
