Is another one essential component in the constant surveillance of its surroundings. The ear and ear of horses have evolved over the years so that it is able to detect the slightest sound that could argue a danger.
Whether they point to the front to focus on something that happens in front, or with one to the front and one to the side to divide their attention, they are a window to the outside of part of what passes through the horse's head. Likewise, other positions indicate emotions: the lowered ears indicate that the horse is down or sick and the backward ears indicate aggressiveness, fear, danger.
The horse is at constant risk of having parasites especially when it eats food and drinks water that is contaminated, hence, the importance of deworming the equine.
While the horse expels many of those parasites through feces in the meadow it is precisely the source where many of the larvae that reproduce at the same time go. By deworming the horse, the cycle of egg reproduction is broken and other possible diseases are avoided.
More than 80% of horses contain these parasites that are located in the large intestine. They live on the surface of the intestinal mucosa and shed large amounts of eggs with their feces.
El pinworm parasite it is the least dangerous and is located in the rectum and large intestine. When the horse has it they permanently rub against the walls, the feeders, etc., and even the horse becomes more irritable.
The history of the domesticated horse inevitably passes through wars. Humanity, since it began to colonize other territories, most of the time has chosen to use force to expand the limits of its world.
