5 What is the current use of warhorses?
Horses have been used in China since the Shang Dynasty (1600-1050 BC). Equine skeletons have been found in this country that were buried together with their owners.
Depending on what he needed it for, the rider would switch from a lighter horse to a heavier one. Thus, for example, the one that was trained to tow a vehicle, used to be lighter; On the other hand, if what he needed to do was fight, he went to a heavier one.
Many cultures have used it, such as the Mongols, Arabs, American Indians, or ancient Egyptians.
From the Iron Age, The first specimens of the Andalusian, Lipizzaner and warm-blooded breeds were used to haul triumphal chariots, supply wagons and to transport relatively heavy weapons, such as light artillery pieces.
Among the heavy horses we find the current percheron, which, although it has a highly developed muscular system, is also very agile on the battlefield.
The mule and the donkey, two animals that today we consider very peaceful and social, have also accompanied human beings in their warlike conflicts. The former, of a much calmer and stronger character than the horse, has been used to transport food and weapons through rough terrain.
