In today's article, we are going to talk about this equine breed with a very characteristic and particular coat: the Appaloosa Horse. You will no doubt agree with me that it is a race that deserves to be among the most beautiful of equines.
Do you want to know a little more about them?
After the reintroduction of equines to America, these they were spreading all over the continent both domesticated horses and maroon horses. The latter are of special importance when it comes to the Appaloosa breed.
For more than 200 years, the Nez Perce selected those horses that had better characteristics and they used the ones that best represented that race that they had in mind. So little by little The attributes that today distinguish Appaloosa horses were established.
The Nez Perce Indians raised their horses for hunting and for war with neighboring tribes, hence it was a strong breed with great resistance during days without hardly drinking and eating. These characteristics have turned them into horses today highly valued in strength and endurance tests, raids, tonnes, etc.
As for the character, we are in front of some equines brave, very energetic and active, a race that excels in his speed and agility.
The speckled coat of the Appaloosa horses is undoubtedly most distinctive and unique feature. But it is important to know that not all spotted horses belong to this breed.
Now let's talk about different types of layers that we can find within this breed:
Snowflake:The base color is dark while the specks on that base are white and are distributed throughout the body.
Spotted loin:This last variety is thought toit could be the original of the race; It consists in that the rump and the kidneys of the animal are light in color and have dark spots.
This breed bred by the Nez Perce was so powerful and tough, that In 1876 the North American government ordered that all Appaloosa horses be exterminated, understanding that they were the best weapon of war of this tribe.
