Have you ever wanted to have a drawing of a horse in a painting?
Perhaps the first answer that comes to mind is "because it is very difficult", but the reality is that it will be as complicated as you want. Yes Yes.
These types of drawings are perfect for decorating children's rooms, brightening up birthday parties and shows. They are also quick to do, since basically consists of drawing geometric figures: a round circle for the head and a smaller one for the muzzle, another oblong for the back, two squares for the ears and four rectangles for the legs.
When you are going to get ready to do it, the first thing we recommend is that look at pictures on the internet, books or encyclopedias of horses. Get familiar with its anatomy; this way it will be easier for you to learn to draw his head, body, tail and the other parts of his body, while in motion or standing still.
Then feel free to print an image that you liked of a horse for reference.
Now, you must create the basic structure of the animal using simple shapes. Draw a large circle or oval for the head, and another two circles for the body (one of them should be about 3 centimeters below and to the right of the head, and the other one should be about 4cm to the side of this one and must be slightly smaller).
Also, feel free to paint it in realistic colors. If the horse you have drawn is black, paint its mane and tail this color; if it is brown, you can paint the mane black and the tail a lighter brown; if it is white, you can paint it all white, or with the mane and / or tail black or brown.
The genuine Tarpan horse or better known as the wild horse
There is not much documentation or even specimens to testify more about its species, being a wild horse that was never tamed let alone leave it in captivity, it is believed that the last free specimen died in 1879. There were two species of the Tarpan or wild horse, the steppe tarpan and the forest tarpan.
The true race of the Tarpan was lost because little by little they were hunted or they were interbreeding with other domestic races, hence there is no real knowledge of when the savage race really disappeared. The last wild tarpan was a mare and died in the Ukraine in December 1879.
