3 What is the body of a horse like?
As with other animals, it is practically impossible to establish general measurements for a horse, since this depends on innumerable factors: breed, sex, state of health, etc. However, based on what has been observed and searched in various sources of information, we are going to offer you some approximate data.
The spine of this animal is made up of 51 vertebrae, and lacks clavicles, so its legs are attached to the trunk by strong muscles and tendons that are held by the scapulae. This peculiarity is also evident, precisely, in the legs and also in the hooves.
This horny tissue is also found in the external area of the hooves, covering them and making them more resistant. Meanwhile, the exterior of these structures is endowed with cartilage and blood tissues such as laminar tissue.
What is the body of a horse like?
Already at the end of the head are the mouth, the nostril and the lip (end of the nose).
Already at the back of the head rests the nape. It should be noted that the entire front of the head is called forehead.
The trunk is formed by the back (top), the chest (located just below the neck, behind the head and is the beginning of the body) and the group (back of the horse). The chest, especially, must be well developed, as a small chest can be synonymous with health problems such as interference in the front legs.
If we move to the hind legs, we have the thigh (which is not more than the upper area of them), the anca (the joint that joins the hind legs with the hips or pelvis) and the buttock (fleshy part located in the upper external area). For their part, the front legs are called legs.
