El Arabian Horse It is one of the oldest breeds, with bloodlines present in most modern racehorse breeds. The most noticeable physical feature is its distinctive head, with prominent eyes, widely spaced and lower nostrils than in other breeds, and wedged shaped snout.
The Arabian horse presents a series of physical and behavioral traits that separate it from other breeds in terms of beauty, style, elegance, dynamism and resistance. Its bone structure is dense and strong, with a unique skeleton based on a singular formation with 17 ribs, 5 lumbar bones and 16 vertebrae in the tail.
A characteristic feature of the Arab race is its tail that gives it a distinguished and elegant touch. Its appearance is arched and with an elevation from the root of it.
Arabian horses are known for their sensitivity, intelligence, an exceptionally noble nature, and a affectionate attitude towards humans. They have great energy that allows them to dominate in endurance races and take part in many fields and equestrian activities other than physical and racing competitions.
Andalusian horses, or Pure Spanish BreedThey are part of our culture that has spread the name of Spain the most throughout the world. A breed admired by all those who love equines.
How could it be otherwise, a land as beautiful and beautiful as Andalusia It had to be the setting where the Andalusian horse was born. The Middle Ages and Caliphate of Córdoba was the triggering context, along with the herds of the upper classes at that time (the biggest culprits).
However, everything could not be a bed of roses, and the rapid growth of the Andalusian horse was interrupted by the War of Independence. Although, once the conflict ended, the activity resumed.
During the Second Republic, the promotion of horse breeding reached the Ministry of Development and Agriculture, promoting it. The work of the Spanish Equestrian Federation was also very important.
It has a very proportionate body, with a medium head, where the most striking are its joyful and tremendously expressive eyes. The chest is really broad and muscular, with a robust back, with a short and broad back.
As for character, this may be his most valuable hallmark. They are docile, noble and intelligent horses, which make them very easy to tame if they are well cared for.
The way in which these animals are raised draws a lot of attention, since it is usually done by a method of semi-freedom in which the mares live in herds. After being born, there are foals that are sold shortly after, and others that remain in the herd until three to four years of age, where they are separated to be tamed.
As always, assigning a certain value to a horse depends on many factors: age, breed, sex, etc. However, it could be said that as a general rule, the value of Andalusian or Purebred Spanish horses is found between the 4000 and the 6000 euros, since it is a breed of great importance.
