Stirrups: Their History, Which One to Choose, and the Right Foot Position | Noti Horses
The stirrups are one of the fundamental elements for the rider when it comes to maintaining balance on the horse. It is an essential piece for the rider's safety, the weight falls on it.
In any of the riding disciplines, foot position must be correct To maintain balance, an essential piece is involved here: the stirrup. You must stomp down and carry the stirrup on the tip of the foot.
Something important to take into account with the stirrups, is never leave them hanging unless you are going to ride, as they can damage the horse hitting against it or getting caught on something. The most convenient is lcarry them tied by sliding them up and passing the action between them to secure them.
Over the years, the stirrups they have been evolving in form and materials. Reaching a point where the competition riders themselves, together with the leading manufacturers in the equestrian market, have developed products where they have put all their personal and professional experiences, increasing safety, comfort and quality.
At some point in the XNUMXth century of our era, were designed and began to be used in China unos supports for the foot that facilitate the support of the rider to the horse. They were performed in cast iron and also some in bronze and they were suspended by straps.
This element, helped the rider to have greater control of the animal to ride. In addition, that being responsible for maintaining the stability of the rider, it provided a greater precision when shooting arrows from the horse and facilitated the use of swords or other weapons.Therefore, this invention was also an improvement in warfare.
The important thing when choosing stirrups is that provide total support to the foot, so that nothing moves at all so that the rider does not have to be concerned with correct foot position and focus on riding. In this sense in Recommended width is approximately 1cm on each side of the foot.
Another interesting detail is that the stirrup have weight, about 3 kg. so that in case they are released they do not get thrown in any direction as with those stirrups that weigh very little.
These stirrups incorporate in their design a rubber tied on the outside so that in the event of a fall the rider's foot is released quickly and easily. If a fall occurs, this rubber will come loose or break releasing the foot.
