Bucephalus, which means bull's head, is the name given to Alexander the Great's horse. This name was given to him since, apparently, he had a rounded face, with a broad forehead where there was also a white spot that, according to it is believed, could have the shape of a star or, perhaps, a bull's head, which is not still very clear.
Plutarch was a historian, philosopher and biographer who was born in Queronea (prefecture of Boeotia, in Rome) during the government of the Roman Emperor Claudius who was born in 46 and died in 127 BC. C. Your version reads like this:
To help him get over it, nothing else occurred to him than to turn his head towards the sun, blinding him. A) Yes, He jumped up and made his father say this phrase"Son, find yourself a kingdom that is equal to your greatness, because Macedonia is small for you."
Pseudo Callisthenes is the name of a book about the life of Alexander the Great whose author is unknown, although it is believed that he was born in Alexandria, Egypt. His version says that Bucephalus was a beautiful horse, but dominated by a wild rage that led him to hunt human beings to eat them, which is known as anthropophagy.
Without a doubt, he must have been a faithful companion, and who knows if a good friend, since Alexander founded in his honor the city of Alexandria Bucephala. This place is believed to be located opposite the modernized town of Jhelum, in the province of Panjab, northeast of Pakistan.
