How to remove ticks from a horse: tips to take care of your horse | Noti Horses
Ticks are parasites that love hot and dry environments, but also feed on the blood of animals (including humans). They can become very dangerous since they also multiply very quickly, so it is important to keep the horse safe from them.
Ticks they are mites that feed on the blood of their victims and also are vectors of infectious diseases. They are characterized by being divided into two parts:
Body (thorax and abdomen fused). Likewise, the reproductive system is located in this area, the female being formed by an ovary, an oviduct and a genital orifice, and the male by testes, seminal vesicles and vas deferens.
Ticks are parasites that we can find in the field, but also in gardens, especially if there are domestic animals, hidden in the grass waiting for a possible victim to pass. Once they locate it, they jump on it and look for the best area from where to start feeding.
During skin piercing, ticks tear blood vessels causing bleeding. At the same time, they introduce the virus or bacteria through their saliva, whose molecules, together with the victim's inflammatory and immune response, cooperate to form an abscess from which they will continue to suck blood.
Ticks, once they fill up, something that happens after a few days, they release on their own. However, For the horse's own safety -and tranquility- it is important to remove them as soon as we have seen them.
With the tweezers in hand, we will hold the ticks by their mouthparts, as close as possible to the animal's skin. Later, we carry out a continuous and slow pulling movement, without rotating, for approximately one minute perpendicular to the skin until we have managed to extract it.
Mix 30ml of olive oil with 10 drops of lavender, eucalyptus and thyme oil.
