The mottled cape of the Appaloosa can be seen in French cave paintings 20.000 years ago. In China, in Ming Dynasty timesThis fur was so precious that painted equine figurines representing these equines have been found.
As in a good number of American breeds, its origin dates back to the horses that the Spanish conquerors brought to America. Many centuries ago, horses had existed in America, but they ended up becoming extinct more than 11.000 million years ago.
The maroon horses were those that were left free, Either because they escaped or because their owners released them during the winter to avoid keeping them or for various other reasons. These equines adapted to the wild life and formed herds throughout America.
It was at this moment of freedom from some spotted horses, when the Nez Perce Indians discovered that that animal with a curious fur, it was adapted to the prototype of animal that they needed for their hunting and war activities. They saw the nobility of these equines, their versatility and strength, to which they had to add their characteristic cape.
With a height between 145 cm and 160 cm, the Appaloosa are considered one of the fastest races that exist today. They present a rounded and very muscular rump, One very strong limbs with bony appearance.
Two details that characterize this breed, in addition to its skin and coat which we will talk about in the next section, are: on the one hand that sclera (the white area of the human eye) is white and more visible than in other breeds of horses in which it can only be seen if the animal looks up, down or to one of the sides. And on the other hand, have vertically striped helmets with light and dark stripes.
Appaloosa have pinkish skin with interspersed dark areas, giving it the appearance of a freckled skin, something that not only it occurs in the body but also on the muzzle, around the eyes and the genital area. To this must be added the aforementioned characteristics of striped helmets and white sclera like humans.
By drop:On a light base, a series of darker colored spots are distributed.
Marbled:In this coat, the front part has a dark base with white and other colored spots, while the rear part of the horse has a light base and dark spots. The limbs are usually dark.
Light loin:The rump and kidney area is light on a dark background. The manes are also dark.
As a general rule, the colors of the coats of the males are more vivid than those of females.
This association established a program for the recovery and improvement of the Appaloosa breed. The few specimens that survived were mixed with the races quarter mile or arabic (from which the AraAppaloosa race arises) for this purpose.
Today the number of registered Appaloosas are over 600.000, making it the third most popular breed in the world.
There are the ticks that feed on equines, attacking their legs, belly and ears, and there are those that are usually found in hedges and brambles from where they pass onto the horse's body, legs and chest. Once there it will feed on your blood.
We will know that the horse has ticks by some very obvious symptoms: will be inappetent, down, high fever and mucus in the eyes and mouth, color between yellow and orange. These will be the symptoms if it is bitten by dozens or hundreds of ticks, since the equine will perceive it as a considerable nuisance, regardless of whether the tick transmits a disease or not.
It is a mistake to try to pull the tick attached to the horse's skin, the head will remain in the skin and that will cause even more acute infections. The intention is that the tick die and fall whole, without leaving any part of the tick inside the horse's skin.
