The mare enters heat in spring or early summer, that is, it is seasonal polyestric, which means that it will never go into heat during the fall or winter. The first time she goes into heat is at 18 months, although it is recommended that she does not mate until she is 3 years old so that she has the first delivery at four years of age.
The female urinates frequently and a mucous discharge comes out of her vagina and, above all, shows a great desire to mate while accepting the male. Regarding the behavior, it can be observed that the mare in the presence of the stallion, shows her ears back, kicks, tries to bite him.
During the time of the estrous cycle, the mare shows her tail raised, clitoral flickering, relaxed cervix and moist genital tract. Follicle stimulating hormone and estrogen predominate.
3 Signs of heat in mares
When we talk about oestrusWe are referring to the reproductive cycle, which we can define as the set of physiological changes and events that occur inside the ovaries in cyclical periods of time due to the variation of hormonal levels.
This estrous cycle lasts a total of 21 days and shows symptoms of heat for five days or so. Ovulation can take place around the penultimate or last day of heat.
It manifests itself in a way loving, placing their ears in a position of attention. His attitude is calm, staying still and always trying to smell the male.
When he is near a stallion, he urinates in order to expel a series of odors to the outside that let the male know that she is receptive. In addition, it also accompanies this urine with a liquid that lubricates your genitals.
He gets into a position where he stretches his hindquarters to tilt the pelvis down. As in the past with the tail, if the stallion is too aggressive it will not do it.
If you do not have a male, it is necessary to keep records of the periods of heat, parturitions and so on.
All this happens around the next 48 hours after ovulation, whether the mare is pregnant or not.
In the same way that happens in females, males also see their behaviors altered when they are in heat, and more if there is a mare close to them.
Another unmistakable sample is the dance performed by the stallions, which consists of drawing a kind of circles at a trot and even making small jumps. This peculiar dance shows the desire of the male to cover the female, but also the strong fear of being rejected.
Normally, a horse will copulate, at most, 3 times in the same day. Although there are times when the mare continues looking for the male with the aim of being covered again, and he agrees again.
