As many other developers, when I discovered Transposh plugin I immediately fell in love with it!
I develop small websites and usually I need to use from 2 to 4 different languages.
The “artisan way” was absolutely boring to me: for each new website I had to repeat every step just to get 2 or 3 flags in my menu.
Today I’m proud to present Language Switcher for Transposh.
it reads Transposh settings and gets the list of languages used in the current website
it allows you to add at the end of the chosen menu(s) a series of flags or a dropdown menu to select the language; Administrators, Authors and Editors will see an Edit translation button too which will allow them to activate the Transposh Translation Editor
if you choose to use a dropdown you can choose if using a select or an unordered list to build your dropdown: I added this option because unordered list give you much more options to customize their look and feel than a select
it allows you set additional classes for your language switcher menu items: this allows you to make it look accordingly to your theme style using the same class your theme is using for navigation menu items
I already have a TODO list to add more features and maybe to create even a premium version, but I think that Language Switcher for Transposh will make your life easier in this first release already.
You can find Language Switcher for Transposh in WordPress.org website (or just searching for “transposh” in the admin dashboard of your WordPress installation): give it a try and feel free to contact me for any issues you can get into.
I will be updating the plugin soon.
The first being a deprecation of old jQuery functions, causing the lazy loader used by the plugin not to function properly.
And I am not really sure if people even bother to optimize their sites any more.
The second major issue was the usage of jQueryUI as the dialog platform that the plugin relies on.
And I was unable to find a suitable dialog alternative.
I will probably just make it work again.
I want to thank everyone who has been supportive of the plugin and its development in the last decade.
See you with a new release that fixes most bugs soon.
This version was held back for way too long but since I finally got the time, it is up and available.
Saving an up to date backup of your human translations is always a good idea.
Yup, this is February 2nd.
Next version will probably include some more versions released by bing translator as well as removing of some old and unused code.
People who opt to pay for support really get a faster response, so feel free to try this.
This decade saw the decline of services providing a free api, but we were able to coup and still provide those services for free.
Enjoy this version and a happy new decade.
Hello everyone, we have released this minor update as we have entered the third decade in which we are operating and supporting this plugin.
We really try to get back to anyone who contacts us using our contact form.
If we go over this decade, many things have changed.
Although still leads to absurd and wrong translations now and than.
In the far past we visited a VC trying to get some funding for this project, and we recall the VC that stated that in 10 years – translation will be a solved issue.
Only shows you that predicting the future is a difficult business.
We will be happy to hear from you, just comment below.
Hi, my website has 1000 articles, every time I upload an article I have to translate all of them?
Maybe you know how long the limits of different translators are updated and part of the content can be translated again?
It really depends on your hosting provider, mainly if other services are using the same server or if that service allows bots to operate from it frequently.
I have been using this plugin for over 5 years, thank you for making it and thank you for continuing to support it.
I really wnt to use this plugin.
And as always – enjoy this version.
Thanks for the compliments, the problem seems that jquery is being loaded twice on the inner pages, which may cause a conflict.
So, what good is it for?
Julien provided me with the information and full disclosure and was very patient with me until I finally had the time to fix everything.
If you are using Google Translate, please use the new button in the utilities tab in order to delete duplicate data.
There are also tons of improvements to the misleading tab called translation editor (which, in hindsight I should have probably called “translation management”) which allows you to have better control and visibility of current translations.
Let me know if you have any thoughts on that.
And includes some minor fixes for bugs encountered in the last year.
I guess he will find more bugs soon and a new release will follow.
We hope you will enjoy this version.
Okay, you already know that so it’s not necessary for me to repeat here why we all love Transposh so much.
I won’t waste your time here to talk about this: if you’re interested you can find a detailed description here The WordPress way
I wanted to get my flags just installing a plugin and maybe adjusting some settings… But that plugin didn’t exist, so I finally decided I had to go beyond my limits, meet the challenge head-on and create my own plugin.
It’s not magic, it doesn’t do miracles but it gets the job done.
So, what Language Switcher for Transposh actually does?
it reads all menu locations available in the current theme and allows you to choose where the language switcher will show up through simple checkboxes
if you choose to use only flags, it allows you to choose between Transposh flags or flags provided by Language Switcher for Transposh itself
if you use an unordered list as dropdown, you can choose if the list items will show flag only, text only or both flags and text
it allows you to totally customize your language switcher using a css editor with syntax highlighting: current stylesheet is loaded in the editor and you can just modify it and then save it or you can even create a totally new css file.
Or at least, this is what I very much hope!
And obviously, if you like it, don’t forget to give it some star (I still didn’t learn how to put in the dashboard those annoying invitations to rating LOL).
I was unable to release new versions of Transposh in the needed frequency, and changes that happened in the wordpress framework has caused parts of the plugin to malfunction.
As there are several issues that are currently bothering users that have upgraded to a more recent wordpress.
This will probably get fixed by either replacing the lazy loader or canceling this feature.
When Transposh was conceived, loading a useless script of 100k seemed a bit much, but the internet has since progressed in speed.
